AB  - The neurophysiological mechanisms of anesthetic-induced loss of unconsciousness (LOC) have been extensively investigated at the macro scale. When it comes to the cortical scale, however, it still remains unclear how the cortical network and the information integration mode change during propofol anesthesia. In this study, we analyzed ECoG data recorded from surgical patients ($\mathrm{n}=9$) with pharmaco-resistant epilepsy during the surgical treatment. We employed the modulation index (MI) to measure the slow oscillation (SO) (0.15-1 Hz)-$\alpha$ (8-13 Hz) phase-amplitude coupling (PAC) of wakefulness to unconsciousness during propofol induced general anesthesia. A method of synchronization measure phase-lag entropy of the diversity of temporal patterns in the phase relationships between two time series was also use to analyze the ECoG changes during propofol anesthesia. Then a genetic algorithm-based support vector machine (GA-SVM) was used to classify the states of consciousness based on the extracted features. The result show that the modulation index of PAC increased in the unconscious state ($\mathrm{p} < 0.001$, t-test), and the PLE decreased significantly after loss of consciousness ($\mathrm{p} < 0.001$, t-test). The GA-SVM showed that the integration of MI and PLE had the highest classification accuracy (93.4%). Our findings provide insight into the couplings of neural oscillations as measures for investigating consciousness by ECoG signals, and also bridge the gap between micro (local field potential) and the macro (electroencephalogram) scale in investigating consciousness during propofol-induced general anesthesia.
