AB  - In order to achieve more accurate classification of various anesthesia consciousness states, this study developed a bimodal EEG-NIRS anesthesia monitoring approach capable of accurately distinguishing three consciousness states during anesthesia (conscious, semi-conscious, and unconscious). Through time-domain Pearson correlation, frequency-domain wavelet coherence, and Granger causality analysis, it was found that prefrontal EEG-NIRS signals exhibited significant differences in neurovascular coupling synchronization and EEG-to-NIRS unidirectional causality patterns across varying anesthesia states. Based on traditional physiological correlation analysis, this study integrated multimodal features, including right prefrontal NIRS-derived frequency-domain phaseamplitude coupling maps and spectral power, temporal linear/nonlinear characteristics, whole-brain scale EEG microstate parameters, and the prefrontal EEG-NIRS bimodal coupling features. The support vector machine (SVM) classifier achieved $\mathbf{8 8. 1 9} \boldsymbol{\pm} \mathbf{2. 3 1 \%}$ classification accuracy, significantly outperforming unimodal approaches, thereby demonstrating the unique advantages of neuroelectric-hemodynamic coupling represented by EEG-NIRS integration for anesthesia monitoring.
