AB  - Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is diagnosed in up to two-thirds of individuals with traumatic brain injury (TBI), yet how cortical responses to OSA vary between sleep states in this population remains poorly understood. We analyzed electroencephalogram (EEG) from polysomnography data of 220 chronic-TBI patients to investigate the spectral alterations of EEG signal between OSA and non-OSA episodes. Artifact-robust Welch analysis of the F3-A2 lead yielded power spectral density estimates, from which seven metrics were computed for each canonical frequency band across NREM and REM sleep states: relative power, spectral entropy, peak frequency, bandwidth, spectral edge frequency at 90% (SEF90), and power coefficient of variation (PowerCV). NREM OSA epochs demonstrated pronounced pathological spectral redistribution with delta power suppression (-2.8%, pFDR<0.001) and broad-spectrum higher frequency enhancement: theta (+8.2%), alpha (+8.1%), beta (+ 15.8%), and gamma (+19.2%) (all pFDR<0.05). Conversely, REM sleep showed markedly attenuated OSA-related alterations with only selective beta band elevation (+4.6%, pFDR=0.020), indicating fundamental sleep state-dependent vulnerability patterns. Spectral complexity analysis revealed altered entropy organization and enhanced PowerCV stability during NREM versus preserved spectral architecture in REM, establishing that TBI creates sleep state-specific neurophysiological dysfunction during respiratory events. These sleep state-dependent spectral signatures reveal unique TBI-OSA pathophysiology and suggest that NREM sleep represents a period of heightened vulnerability while REM mechanisms provide partial cortical protection. This injury-specific spectral fingerprint, detectable with singlechannel EEG using non-parametric statistical analysis, may serve as a potential biomarker for screening TBI-OSA comorbidity and indicates that sleep state-targeted treatments may optimize outcomes for brain-injured populations.
