AB  - The brain is a complex system requiring multimodal approaches to better understand cognitive or motor functions. Thus, different and complementary electroencephalographic (EEG) neurophysiological features are available at various spatial, frequency, and temporal scales, e.g., brain connectivity, complexity, or entropy. However, they are usually not investigated all together. In this study, we combine and compare five EEG-based connectivity features with covariance matrices, defining five Riemannian fusion methods and three Euclidean ones as references. We do so for classifying motor imagery EEG signals, both in awake and sedated subjects, with the future goal of detecting accidental awareness during general anesthesia. Covariance matrices alone yielded the best accuracy, with and without sedation. Phase-based connectivity estimators appear to be the most promising fusion with covariances. No significant differences were found between the best fusion of features and that of classifiers.
