AB  - Alzheimer’s Disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder and detecting AD at its early stages is a challenging task. Advances in EEG signal processing techniques like entropy-based methods aid in detecting abnormalities in brain activities with higher accuracy. Entropy-based machine learning algorithms can detect minute changes in brain activity caused by AD and Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI). Recent studies on machine learning algorithms like Support Vector Machines (SVM) report accuracy of more than 90% in AD classification. Also, researchers have found proof that Large Language Models (LLMs) can be used to detect AD. By detecting certain changes in speech patterns, languages cues as well as other multimodal data like audio, LLMs can help spot subtle signs of cognitive decline. For early detection of AD, longitudinal assessment of cognitive health is required. In this paper we propose a framework that uses brain waves collected using wearable EEG devices as well as heart rate variability (HRV) and sleep data from other wearables for assessing cognitive health. The proposed system will leverage LLMs to derive entropy-based complexity values from EEG as well as HRV signals. Analysis of sleep data can help in tracing the signs of cognitive decline. These multimodal inputs can help the AI system continuously monitor an individual’s health and provide them with a daily report.
