AB  - Electroencephalogram (EEG) signals captured during surgical procedure can be used for estimation of depth of anesthesia (DoA). Several features can be extracted from such EEG signals. The features extracted could be of Frequency Domain, Time Domain, Hjorth Parameter, Entropy, Statistical Parameter, Complexity Parameter or Clinical Parameter. Calculation of co-relation between the features extracted from EEG and that of DoA index and analysis of their importance will play a vital role in proper estimation DoA index. In this paper, five spectral features, viz., Spectral Edge Frequency 95% (SEF95), Spectral Energies of Alpha, Beta, Delta and Theta bands (SEA, SEB, SED, SET) are extracted from EEG data along with DoA index (i.e. Bispectral Index) from 100 patient’s data available in VitalDB. Statistical co-relations viz. Pearson, Spearman and Kendall’s Tau in between DoA index (Bispectral Index) and these features are calculated. The Spectral Edge Frequency 95 (SEF95) showed the strongest correlation with DoA index followed by SED. A Random Forest Model for DoA estimation is also developed using these five EEG features and trained on 100 patients’ data. The feature importance and occurrence of features as root feature, obtained from the model, revealed SEF95 as the most important feature. Finally these features are trained on several supervised machine learning models namely K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Decision Tree, Naive Bayes, Random Forest, and Support Vector Machine (SVM). The training result shows the Random Forest model performed the best with testing accuracy of 0.85.
