AB  - This study presents an automated approach for classifying sleep stages in mice using EEG and EMG signals. A Random Forest model was trained on features extracted from preprocessed polysomnography data, which were matched to expert-labeled sleep stages. The proposed classifier achieved a balanced accuracy of $87.81 \% \pm 6.46 \%$ on Dataset $A(n=20$ subjects) and $80.38 \% \pm 3.39 \%$ on Dataset $B(n=7$ subjects) using subject-independent $\mathbf{1 0}$-fold and $\mathbf{7}$-fold cross-validation, respectively. Analysis of feature importance showed EMGbased features and delta power were consistently the most informative features across both datasets, with good stability across cross-validation folds. The difference in model performance between datasets suggests preprocessing-specific effects. Future research will aim to address these limitations by refining preprocessing methods, improving REM detection through feature engineering or class-specific strategies, optimizing model parameters, and incorporating deep learning techniques such as recurrent neural networks. Overall, this work represents an initial step in investigating both the potential and current hurdles in the development of automated sleep stage classifiers for rodent studies.
