AB  - Sleep disorders particularly insomnia has significantly impact mental and physical health. Traditional diagnostic methods like polysomnography (PSG) are resource-intensive and rely on manual evaluation, limiting efficiency. By focusing on CAP Phase B, a critical recovery phase during sleep, the model enhances diagnostic precision, capturing subtle transitions and disruptions associated with sleep disorders. To tackle this issue, we propose a deep learning approach-based classification framework utilizing multimodal physiological signals EEG, EMG, and ECG. Three hybrid model like CNNLSTM, CNN-BiGRU, and CNN-BiLSTM are developed to enhance spatial and temporal feature extraction. The models are trained and evaluated using the CAP Sleep Database from PhysioNet. Among them, the CNN-BiLSTM model attains a top classification accuracy of (99.99%) outperforming CNN-BiGRU (96.69%) and CNN LSTM (94.46%). The integration of convolutional and bidirectional networks significantly improves detection accuracy, making the proposed method a promising tool for automated insomnia diagnosis.
