AB  - Drowsiness has long been recognized as a major cause of accidents and reduced performance in safety-critical fields such as transportation, aviation, and industrial operations. EEG signals provide a non-invasive and dependable method of monitoring the patterns of brain activity that are related to alertness and fatigue. However, Existing detection approaches are however ineffective in capturing the spatial and temporal dynamics of EEG data which limits classification accuracy, as well as real-time applicability. In a bid to overcome this drawback, our work suggests a hybrid deep-learning architecture that combines the features of the Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) to extract spatial features and Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) networks to model the time series. EEG recordings were automatically processed, split into 5-second epochs, and categorized into four physiologically significant sleep stages (W, N1, N2, and N3). Accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score were used to assess performance. Automatic sleep stage classification is a validated physiological proxy for drowsiness modeling in safetycritical systems because real-world drowsiness correlates neurologically to the transitional boundary between wakefulness (W) and light sleep (N1). The proposed model achieved an overall accuracy of 88.28% for multi-class classification across the four sleep-stage alertness levels: W, $\mathrm{N} 1, \mathrm{N} 2$, and N 3. The findings show that the suggested hybrid framework offers a dependable and efficient method for classifying sleep stages based on EEG for drowsiness modeling, with great potential for integration into intelligent fatigue monitoring and safety-support systems.
