AB  - Sleep apnea is a common sleep disorder that often goes unnoticed in individuals, but it can have serious consequences. It can cause high blood pressure and negatively affect cardiovascular and brain vascular functions, which can exacerbate health issues. The gold standard diagnostic method, Polysomnography (PSG), simultaneously measures various signals, including EEG, EMG, ECG, EOG, SpO2, and respiratory signals during an overnight stay at a hospital. However, it is both inconvenient and resource-intensive. In this study, we proposed an alternative method utilizing features from ECG signals, such as ECG entropy and other non-linear features, along with effective features from respiratory signals. We trained and tested our model based on the XGBOOST with data from the Sleep-Heart-Health-Study-2 database. In addition, we used the feature selection strategy to identify the most significant features. As a result, our model achieved performance with an accuracy of 68.83%, a precision of 71.61%, and a recall of 67.84% when testing our model with previously unseen data from 422 participants. These results suggest that our model has the potential to efficiently and accurately detect sleep apnea.
