AB  - Sleep stage classification is an essential part in the diagnosis of sleep disorders. This paper presents an enhanced sleep stage classification model fueled with multi-channel electroencephalography (EEG) signals. The proposed model is consisted of two modules: a spiking neural network for encoding and a transformer for classification. The spiking network has two layers of leaky integrate-and-fire neurons, and between the layers, one inhibitory neuron and one de-inhibitory neuron are added to remove artifacts with EEG. This paper further develops hybrid synaptic weights to extract multi-channel features, where trainable weights are optimized by a spike-timing dependent plasticity (STDP) algorithm and fixed ones are added to stabilize the network. Two types of datasets are used to validate the proposed model: Sleep-EDF and ISRUC-Sleep. It is shown that the model has an accuracy above 85% and a precision over 92% in N 2 and N3 stages. The ablation experiments demonstrate that multichannel spiking network can significantly improve the model performance. Moreover, based on the spiking encoder, a data indicator is calculated to quantify sleep scoring, as an interpretable index of our sleep stage classification model.
