AB  - Sleep is an essential physiological function vital for neural regulation, cognitive performance, and emotional stability. The accurate identification of sleep stages and detection of sleep disorders are critical in clinical diagnostics and neurocognitive research. Manual sleep scoring from polysomnography (PSG) recordings is labor-intensive and inconsistent among expert scorers. This study introduces Fusion-AttnNet, a hybrid EEG-based framework for automated classification of sleep stages and disorders. The method integrates multi-domain EEG features with deep learning techniques incorporating temporal attention mechanisms. The architecture combines convolutional feature extractors, Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) layers and a self-attention fusion module to learn spatial-spectral representations and temporal transitions. Unlike traditional CNN- or RNN-based methods, Fusion-AttnNet merges multidomain handcrafted descriptors with deep temporal attention in a hybrid framework, enhancing physiological sleep transition learning while maintaining interpretability. Testing on Sleep-EDF and CAP databases showed 93.2% accuracy $(\kappa=0.88)$ for sleep stage classification and 89.4% accuracy $(\kappa=\mathbf{0. 8 4})$ for sleep disorder classification. The model outperformed baseline methods like ResNet18, EfficientSleepNet, and XGBoost. Attention heatmaps and activation analyses demonstrated interpretable learned features. The framework provides an interpretable solution for automated sleep analysis applicable to clinical decision support systems.
