AB  - This study presents a comparative analysis of puretone and self-name auditory stimuli through EEG signal processing and machine learning techniques, aiming to quantify their differential effects on attentional capture and semantic processing. High-density EEG recordings ($10-20$ system) were preprocessed using band-pass filtering ($0.1-40 ~\text{Hz}$), independent component analysis (ICA) for artifact removal, and average reference reconstruction. A $\mathbf{1 6}$-dimensional feature setincluding time-domain measures (peak amplitude and latency), frequency-domain metrics (power spectrum), and nonlinear entropy assessments (sample entropy and approximate entropy)was extracted. Sliding-window data augmentation was employed to balance class distributions between the two stimulus conditions. Classification was performed using Random Forest, Support Vector Machine (SVM), K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN), and Decision Tree algorithms, which revealed significantly higher accuracy for self-name stimuli at $\mathbf{8 2. 9 \%}$, compared to $\mathbf{7 6. 4 \%}$ for pure-tone stimuli $(\mathbf{p}<0.01)$. Event-related potential analyses further demonstrated enhanced mismatch negativity (MMN) and P300 amplitudes under the self-name condition, measuring $-7.62 \mu ~\mathrm{V}$ at 203 ms and $3.72 \mu ~\mathrm{V}$ at 367 ms, respectively. These results underscore the unique salience of self-name stimuli in engaging attentional and semantic networks. The findings suggest that selfname stimuli may hold promising utility in future cognitive neuroscience research as well as clinical applications, including consciousness assessment, attention disorder screening, and neurorehabilitation planning.
