AB  - Mental illnesses are highly related to sleep disorders, and although the brain signals are complicated, real-time monitoring poses a challenge. The paper is an IoT-enabled project of sleep state and mental health monitoring using EEG signals. EEG-based wearables continuously record the brain, which is used with four novel inferences Baptized as Quantum-Inspired Phase Pattern Extraction (QIPPE) to detect micro-state transitions, Hierarchical Temporal Attention Mapping (HTAM) to detect adaptive weightings of features, Multiscale Nonlinear Entropy Fusion (MNEF) to detect anomalies, and Graph-Convolutional Sleep State Prediction (GC-SSP) to predict inter-channel connectivity. The detection of sleep stages and mental health abnormalities proposed is accurate with low latency. The experimental findings show a higher accuracy rate of detection compared to the traditional methods and the absence of challenges when implementing the IoT to monitor remote environments. It is a framework that allows a customized mental health since EEG can be translated into actionable data, which can be used as a groundbreaking tool to conduct early intervention and ongoing monitoring.
