AB  - Emotional state recognition is essential for mental health assessment and the development of intelligent healthcare systems. This study proposes an EEG-based emotion recognition framework for smart healthcare automation, utilizing non-linear fractal features from the DEAP dataset. Two descriptors: Higuchi Fractal Dimension (HFD) and Petrosian Fractal Dimension (PFD) were extracted from 32-channel EEG signals to capture complex brain dynamics. Ensemble models, including Light Gradient Boosting Machine (Light-GBM) and Random Forest, were applied for binary classification of valence and arousal. After preprocessing (bandpass filtering, 3 -second segmentation, $\mathbf{Z}$ score normalization and binary labeling), results showed that HFD outperformed PFD, achieving 82.47% and 80.62% accuracy for valence and arousal with Light-GBM. Independent-samples t-tests confirmed the statistical significance of these differences. The findings highlight HFD's superior ability to model EEG non-linearity, offering a robust approach for emotion-aware healthcare applications such as mental health monitoring, neurofeedback and personalized smart systems.
