AB  - Alzheimer's disease (AD) presents a significant challenge to global healthcare due to its devastating individual impact and substantial economic burden. Existing diagnostic methods, including clinical evaluations and neuroimaging techniques like electroencephalography (EEG), offer limited accuracy, hindering early detection and timely intervention. This study introduces a novel convolutional neural network-long short-term memory-attention architecture for the accurate classification of AD, frontotemporal dementia, and cognitively normal individuals. Using open-source EEG resting-state data, this method precisely captures complex cognitive response patterns, addressing the heterogeneity in AD presentation. A comprehensive set of features, including fractional dimensions, Lyapunov exponents, spectral entropy, and power spectral density, are extracted, enabling robust EEG signal analysis. The proposed architecture demonstrates superior performance, achieving an accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score of 97.4, 97.44, 97.42, and 97.43 %, respectively, offering significant potential for improved AD diagnosis.
