AB  - Electroencephalography (EEG) plays an essential role in emotion studies due to its ability to capture emotional signals in an objective manner. Despite significant advancements in EEG-based emotion recognition, capturing and integrating the relationships between EEG features across different domains remains a challenging issue. To exploit the multi-dimensional characteristics of EEG signals, this research introduces a new emotion recognition model called the Multi-Dimensional Fusion Transformer (MDFT). The framework combines a 3D convolutional neural network (3D-CNN) with a transformer-based mechanism to achieve fusion of features across multiple dimensions. First, EEG signals are filtered to extract five specific frequency bands for each channel. Differential entropy (DE) features are subsequently extracted from each frequency band and temporally synchronized across the EEG channels. These features are organized into a three-dimensional time-frequency-channel fused EEG map. Next, a 3D-CNN-based module is applied to extract localized multi-dimensional features from the fused map. The generated four-dimensional feature tensor is restructured into a two-dimensional representation. Finally, the processed representations are delivered to the self-attention layers of the transformer, where the multi-head attention mechanism is applied to capture the dependencies among features from different domains. These outputs are then regarded as key emotion-relevant features for the final classification process. The model achieves high classification accuracies on the DEAP dataset, with approximately 99.25% for arousal and 99.19% for valence. It also attains roughly 98.40% accuracy on the SEED dataset for emotion classification, further demonstrating the model’s effectiveness in recognizing emotions from EEG signals.
