AB  - Electroencephalography (EEG) signals are often contaminated by artifacts, with eye-blink artifacts being a major source of noise that can significantly affect analysis. Independent Component Analysis (ICA) is one of the most popular techniques used for artifact removal; however, it usually requires manual inspection and removal of artifact-related components, making the process subjective and time-consuming. This paper presents two novel hybrid approaches that integrate ICA, wavelet denoising, and Machine Learning techniques for fully automated eye-blink artifact removal. These methods take the traditional approach of ICA-based methodology by incorporating wavelet denoising and classification by Machine Learning(ML) for less human intervention with an increased accuracy. A comparative analysis of the conventional ICA method and our proposed hybrid techniques is conducted using quantitative metrics, including Higuchi Fractal Dimension (HFD), Shannon Entropy, Mean Squared Error (MSE), and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE). Experimental results show that our hybrid techniques outperform the conventional technique in both artifact reduction and signal integrity, making them more efficient and reliable for EEG preprocessing.
