AB  - While multi-channel EEG and EOG provide comprehensive data, the large number of electrodes limits their feasibility for long-term home-based monitoring. Therefore, reducing the number of channels is essential to improve feasibility for real-world applications. Considering the nonstationary EEG and EOG signals, which exhibit both periodic and non-periodic characteristics, we employed both linear and nonlinear correlation measures in our channel selection strategies. In this study, five index were applied to evaluate inter-channel relationships: Pearson correlation, cross-correlation, cosine similarity, mutual information, and dynamic time warping. Additionally, a CNN-LSTM model was used to assess sleep stage scoring performance. Experimental results showed that the reduced channel sets (linear: O1, E1, E2; nonlinear: C4, O2, E1) resulted in a decrease in overall accuracy of only $1.83 \%-3.45 \%$, while training and testing times were shortened by approximately one-third. Importantly, recognition rates for deep sleep and wakefulness remained high. These findings demonstrate that the proposed channel reduction strategy effectively balances accuracy and computational efficiency, improving the practicality and potential clinical value of home-based sleep monitoring.
