AB  - Emotion recognition based on electroencephalography (EEG) is an important research topic in affective computing and brain–computer interface (BCI) systems. To mitigate the limited recognition accuracy, a machine-learning-based EEG emotion recognition method is developed by integrating multi-feature representation and supervised classification. Firstly, EEG signals acquired from the frontal-pole electrodes FP1, FP2, and Fz are preprocessed using low-pass filtering, wavelet reconstruction, and α-band extraction to enhance emotion-related components. Subsequently, multidimensional features, including wavelet packet decomposition (WPD), power spectral density (PSD), and sample entropy (SampEn), are extracted to construct discriminative feature vectors. On this basis, a Support Vector Machine (SVM) classifier is employed to model the emotion classification task, with k-fold cross-validation and grid-search strategies applied for parameter optimization and generalization performance evaluation. Experiments conducted on EEG data collected from 56 participants achieve an average recognition accuracy of 82.3% across two emotional classification valence and arousal. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed machine-learning-based method can effectively exploit emotion-discriminative information in EEG signals, providing a reliable solution for intelligent affective analysis and human–machine interaction applications.
