AB  - Epileptic EEG signals exhibit non-stationary and nonlinear characteristics. Studying the complexity of epileptic electroencephalography(EEG) signals can reflect the orderliness and synchronization of brain neural system activity. This study investigated the complexity of epileptic EEG signals from three dimensions: temporal, spatial, and spatiotemporal. Furthermore, the study calculated the differences in EEG signal complexity across different frequency bands. The results of the study revealed a significant decrease in temporal and spatial complexity during epileptic seizures, while spatiotemporal complexity showed a significant increase. The results indicate that the complexity of epileptic EEG can effectively distinguish different stages of epilepsy, providing strong support for future research on epilepsy detection and prediction, with significant research value.
