AB  - The causal dynamics of covariates of emotional responses and EEG brain wave signals offer implications for psychology, user experience design, and human-computer interaction. With advances in brain-machine interaction, machine learning, and emotion analysis, the study employs a comprehensive framework that combines linear regression and structural equation modelling to identify key covariates that influence emotional valence and arousal, fundamental dimensions of emotions. This research study uses Dataset for Emotion Analysis using Physiological Signals (DEAP) with EEG recordings, time-stamped observations, and emotional labels. The results show a robust causal relationship between EEG wave recordings and emotional valence with varying accuracy in predicting arousal. The Instrumentation Variable (IV) Estimation values provide causal dynamics of EEG brain waves with Valence and Arousal using adjusted R-squared values, log-likelihood and Information Criteria (MAE(0.02 to 0.19), adjusted R-Squared(0.21 to 0.91), negative AIC/BIC scores(> -153800.00)) The results contribute to the understanding of the complex interplay between neural activity patterns and emotional states, with implications for refining psychological interventions, optimizing user experiences, and promoting symbiotic human-computer interactions.
