AB  - This paper introduces a novel EEG emotion recognition model based on the Mixture of Experts (MoE) framework. The model addresses the challenges in cross-subject EEG emotion recognition by incorporating a leader model and multiple expert models. Utilizing K-means clustering, the preprocessed EEG data are segmented into distinct subsets, with each expert model tasked with processing a subset of data with similar features, while the leader model oversees the entire dataset. Furthermore, an encoder-decoder architecture is designed for each expert model, primarily consisting of spatiotemporal convolutions and LSTM networks, aimed at reconstructing EEG signals and deriving a reconstruction loss to get the weights of the expert model. The experimental results using leave-one-out cross-validation on the DEAP public dataset indicate that the proposed MoE-based approach outperforms single-model methodologies across various evaluation metrics, offering an innovative and effective solution for emotion recognition.
