AB  - A chronic neurological condition called epilepsy is typified by abrupt, erratic seizures brought on by aberrant brain electrical activity. These seizures may cause injury or other consequences and can vary in severity from mild consciousness loss to severe convulsions. Improving patient safety and care requires quick and precise seizure detection. Electroencephalogram (EEG) recordings are frequently used to track and investigate epilepsy; nevertheless, manual interpretation of these signals is laborious, subjective, and necessitates ongoing professional supervision. In this study, we suggest an automatic method for classifying EEG segments as seizure or non-seizure based on Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). The CHB-MIT Scalp EEG Database, a well-known standard in epilepsy research, was used to train the model. Experimental results indicate that our approach achieves an accuracy of 99%, highlighting its robustness and generalization capability. This framework has the potential to support real-time monitoring and alert systems, thereby reducing diagnostic delays and minimizing the risks associated with epileptic seizures.
