AB  - Accurate sleep stage classification is important in diagnosis and monitoring of numerous sleep disorders. In this paper, we present a research proposal, which is a multimodal structure of sleep stage detection using automated methods with the Sleep-EDF Expanded data. The $\text{Fpz}-\text{Cz}$ and $\text{Pz}-\text{Oz}$ EEG signals were converted to three complementary time-frequency representations Spectrograms through Short Time Fourier Transform (STFT), Continuous Wavelet Transforms (CWT) and HilbertHuang Transforms (HHT). All the representations were processed by a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that incorporated the spatial feature extraction properties of CNN with the properties of the Transformer to predict the temporal relationships. Also, a multi-branch network is constructed to be able to process different input representations simultaneously. The model combines these properties into a composite predictor which is much better in terms of generalisation and robustness. The experimental findings prove better results on six stages of sleep (Wake, N1, N2, N3, N4 and REM) with spectrogram, CWT, HHT, and multi-input models accuracies of $92.49 \%, 97.00 \%, 97.30 \%$, and $\mathbf{9 8. 7 9 \%}$, respectively. These results highlight the effectiveness of multimodal fusion and hybrid designs in the capture of complementary spectral characteristics which facilitate efficient and interpretable identification of sleep stages.
