AB  - Emotion recognition has great importance for communication between people as well as interactions between human and machine towards various applications. Brain signal EEG is a significant marker of emotion, especially for people unfit to communicate or express their emotions through posture or gesture. This paper presents a 3-class emotion (Positive, Neutral and Negative) classification framework emphasizing on broad range of optimized features and utilizing the long-term dependency benefit of extended LSTM (xLSTM) learning. From a set of 2132×2549 features extracted from EEG signals in the publicly available EEG Brainwave Dataset, an optimized 2132×200 feature set was selected using the Minimum Redundancy Maximum Relevance (mRMR) method. An xLSTM based network has been designed focusing on the memory module mLSTM and the sLSTM module to address the storing capacity issue of LSTM. The exponential gating and innovative memory mixing structure of this model enhances its performance compared to the traditional LSTM model in terms of long-term dependencies. The obtained results demonstrate that the proposed framework outperforms conventional LSTM and related works exhibiting of 99.06% of accuracy where the LSTM shows an accuracy of 97.17% on test data. The outcomes of this study might be useful in emotion related applications for human machine interaction, reminiscence therapy, robotics etc.
