AB  - Due to advances in Brain-Computer Interfacing (BCI) technology, easy to operate non-invasive electroencephalographic (EEG) and electrooculographic (EOG) headsets are now available for the general consumer. This has made developing applications aimed at human subjects, with partial or total loss of motor control feasible, on an economical scale. In this paper, we present a BCI application for drawing simple two-dimensional line-images. We make use of instantaneous information on eyeblink detection, attention and meditation levels, as provided by a NeuroSky MindWave Mobile headset, to control a pointer's movement on a user's screen, similar to moving a pen/pencil on paper. No limb movement is involved in the process, and our application can closely emulate the organic manner of writing and drawing for differently-abled users, giving them more control than existing applications in a cost-effective, non-invasive manner.
