AB  - The large-amplitude, occipital alpha rhythm of the human electroencephalogram (EEG) is associated in normal subjects with a state of relaxed wakefulness. We analyzed resting eyes-open and eyes-closed human EEG data using three measures: power in the 8-12-Hz alpha frequency band, estimated correlation dimension (D2), and the Savit-Green S-statistic. At occipital loci, two groups of points were evident in the scatterplot of S vs. estimated D2. Group A was of higher dimension and consisted of predominantly eyes-open records. Group B was of lower dimension and consisted of more eyes-closed than eyes-open records. Furthermore, Group B had a broad range of alpha power, with alpha power being negatively correlated with estimated D2 (higher alpha power associated with lower dynamical complexity). In contrast, Group A had a very small range of low alpha power, and was positively correlated with estimated D2 (higher alpha power associated with increased dynamical complexity). Our results indicate that Group B EEG (the alpha rhythm) and Group A EEG (EEG containing 'other' alpha activity) have fundamentally different dynamical properties.
DO  - 10.1016/s0167-8760(97)00769-1
