AB  - OBJECTIVES: To investigate the dynamic functional connectivity in narcolepsy type 1 (NT1) patients and whether clinical features are associated with abnormal connectivity. METHODS: We investigated dynamic functional connectivity (dFC) of 80 drug-naïve NT1 patients, 30 idiopathic hypersomnia (IH) patients and 60 age- and sex-matched healthy controls using sliding-window approach, clustering state analysis and graph-theory methods. Relationships between clinical features and abnormal dFC properties were evaluated. RESULTS: Three dFC brain states were identified to characterize and compare dynamic functional connectivity patterns: a more frequent and sparsely connected State I (46.96%), a less frequent and more strongly connected State Ⅱ (26.3%), and a less frequent and sparsely connected State Ⅲ (26.74%). In NT1 patients, the occurrence of the State Ⅱ increased, corresponding with a prolonged dwell time of State Ⅱ. While State Ⅲ are predominant for IH patients and healthy controls. The occurrence and dwell time of State Ⅲ are negatively correlated with sleep latency in NT1 patients. Additionally, there was a higher variability in the small-world network parameters, suggesting an abnormal global integration of the brain network in both IH and NT1 patients. CONCLUSIONS: The abnormal temporal patterns and functional segregation coupled with the severity of sleepiness and abnormal local integration in topological properties confirmed the vulnerability of functional networks in NT1 patients.
DO  - 10.1016/j.clinph.2025.2110821
