AB  - The microvascular decompression procedure (MVD) is widely utilized on patients with neurovascular compression syndromes, such as trigeminal neuralgia, hemifacial spasm and glossopharyngeal neuralgia, which have failed medical therapy. However, surgical complications are an ongoing problem. We retrospectively reviewed a total of 596 patients undergoing MVD in the Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University from January 2008 to December 2018. Furthermore, we discussed the cases with life-threatening complications to determine the potential causes, aiming to achieve the goal of safer microvascular decompression. There were seven cases with life-threatening complications. Of those complications, one was cerebellar infarction with acute hydrocephalus, one was infarction of the cerebellum and the brain stem with acute hydrocephalus and serious intracranial infection, two were cerebellar haematoma, one was multiple haemorrhage with acute hydrocephalus, one was supratentorial subdural haematoma, and one was cerebellar swelling with acute hydrocephalus. After therapy, one patient died, one was in a persistent vegetative state, and five were discharged from the hospital upon recovery. In brief, MVD is a safe operation, and life-threatening complications accompanying MVD are rare, but require attention. The causes of some life-threatening complications are still not completely clear. Surgeons should continuously improve surgical techniques and perioperative care to reduce potential risks.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jocn.2021.01.014
