AB  - Tension pneumocephalus is a neurosurgical emergency that occurs when air is trapped in the intracranial cavity, leading to brain compression and causing severe neurological symptoms such as decreases in motor function, sensory function, and consciousness. Most cases of pneumocephalus require conservative treatment; however, because of the possible fatal complications, rapid diagnosis and appropriate treatment are important. Here, we present the case of an 81-year-old male patient who had undergone head trauma three hours prior to being admitted to our emergency room (ER) because of mental cloudiness. The radiologic findings showed tension pneumocephalus caused by an ethmoidal roof fracture. Emergency reconstruction of the ethmoidal roof with craniotomy was performed to remove the intracranial air using normal saline irrigation. By sharing our experience with this case, we hope to provide an option for the treatment of such cases.
DO  - 10.3390/diagnostics13010092
