AB  - Quantifying the brain's level of - and capacity for consciousness has major implications for the assessment of unresponsive patients. The brain is an inherently dynamic organ, whose capacity for consciousness requires the ability to continuously react and adapt to environmental changes. Perturbation has long been a key concept for the assessment of human consciousness. However, the requirement of specialized stimulation techniques, and contraindications for patients who necessitate sedation poses barriers towards the implication of existing indices in the intensive care unit. In this thesis, we outline different pathways and propose novel techniques to better understand brain mechanisms underlying consciousness and to improve assessment of unresponsive patients. First, we explore how pharmacologically- and pathologically- altered states of consciousness are reflected in brain dynamics, estimated through spectral properties, complexity, and criticality of the human electroencephalography (EEG). Second, we demonstrate how altered brain dynamics modulate the response to anesthetic and electromagnetic perturbation. Combining current approaches with EEG priors could optimize and guide perturbational techniques. Third, we investigate the value of anesthesia as a global perturbation for the assessment of unresponsive patients. Being largely available in intensive care units, the use of EEG and anesthesia has high translatability for clinical practice. In the last part of this thesis, we propose a novel clinical tool which complements behavioral assessment during interruption of sedation to provide clinicians with a perspective on the neurological aspect of 'waking up'. Assessing an unresponsive patient's level of consciousness has significant implications for clinical teams in determining treatment, setting realistic goals, and providing family with adequate information. This thesis contributes towards improvement of the clinical assessment of unresponsive patients and the further understanding of the brain mechanisms which give rise to consciousness. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
