KW  - *Dissociation
KW  - *Electrical Activity
KW  - *Electroencephalography
KW  - *Ketamine
KW  - *Machine Learning
AB  - Understanding consciousness requires clarifying how the brain supports two fundamental processes: the ability to support internal experience and the capacity to respond to external stimuli. These processes are typically concurrently modulated, complicating efforts to disentangle their neural underpinnings. The critical brain hypothesis, which posits that conscious brains self-tune to a critical point poised between stability and instability which optimizes information processing and responsiveness, provides a first-principles framework to examine how the brain may become unresponsive despite preserved internal experience. Ketamine, a dissociative anesthetic, readily induces a state of reduced behavioral responsiveness while producing vivid dream-like experiences, making it an ideal pharmacological tool for this inquiry. This dissertation employs a quantitative approach, combining high-density electroencephalography (EEG), linear stability analysis, machine-learning classifiers, and psychophysical assays to systematically investigate ketamine-induced dissociation. First, EEG data from healthy human volunteers receiving escalating doses of ketamine were analyzed to track the proximity of cortical dynamics to criticality. The results reveal a dose-dependent stabilization of cortical dynamics, signifying a departure from dynamical criticality as behavioral responsiveness is gradually reduced. Stability metrics greatly outperformed traditional spectral analyses in predicting ketamine dose, implicating dynamical stabilization as a robust neurophysiological marker of unresponsiveness. In the same sample, audiovisual sensory integration was assessed using the sound-induced flash illusion (SIFI), a well-established psychophysical paradigm, to quantitatively characterize perceptual changes under varying ketamine doses. Linear mixed effects modeling demonstrated dose-dependent heightening of multisensory integration, and a lengthening of the window during which stimuli are summed to form a single percept. This work advances our fundamental understanding of dissociative conscious states and offers practical implications for guiding dosing in clinical applications involving ketamine administration. Lastly, I synthesize my work with prior studies applying statistical criticality approaches to support the critical brain hypothesis as a general framework by which we can understand consciousness in terms of its component processes. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
