AB  - Sleep deprivation in adults deteriorates the small-world organization of intrinsic brain networks potentially compromising the brain's performance capability. Children and adolescents are particularly exposed to sleep loss due to biological and societal pressures. We test an unexamined hypothesis that inattention or hyperactivity traits associated with attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) may expose vulnerability to sleep loss. This hypothesis is supported by the sleep-loss-like brain state in ADHD which may predispose to greater impairment after a homeostatic challenge. Limited research has examined sleep loss and brain function in adolescents; studies that exist skew to older neurotypical youth free of ADHD-like traits. By restricting samples, the field is challenged to identify whether some children may respond disproportionately to the sleep loss that many school-aged children and adolescents routinely experience. Here we present preliminary evidence from a within-subject study of wake extension in a small sample of 10-13-year-old youth on a range of inattention and hyperactivity. We hypothesized that children with higher inattention and hyperactivity symptoms would experience greater alterations in brain connectivity and behavioral performance after wake extension (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
