AB  - Comments on an article by Jakub Vohryzek et al. (see record 2026-65487-001). Connectome harmonics (CH) is a novel fMRI-based technique that decomposes spatial patterns of brain activity into a series of connectome frequencies, derived from the brain's structural wiring. This approach is conceptually like Fourier analysis, but instead of time-based frequencies, CH uses the eigenmodes of the structural connectome. A recent study by Vohryzek et al. applied CH analysis to characterize the neural dynamics underlying the N,N-dimethyltryptamine (DMT) experience. The experimental design used a within-subject, placebo-controlled, single-blind protocol to maximize sensitivity to drug-induced changes while controlling for inter-individual variability. This study is the first to use CH to characterize the structurefunction dynamics of the human brain under the influence of DMT using fMRI, combined with EEG temporal dynamics. The findings reveal a robust and reproducible shift toward highfrequency harmonics, a reduction in low-frequency harmonics, and a corresponding increase in neural entropy, effects that are strongly correlated with the intensity of subjective psychedelic experience. Overall, Vohryzek et al.provide compelling evidence that CH analysis offers a powerful tool for quantifying psychedelic effects on brain dynamics and linking them to subjective experience. This approach holds promise for advancing our understanding of altered states of consciousness across a range of psychoactive substances. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1038/s41386-025-02217-w
