AB  - Comments on an article by Sean Tanabe et al.(see record 2022-45565-014). The study by Tanabe et al., which demonstrated reduced electroencephalographic (EEG) complexity, assessed via the Lempel-Ziv algorithm, during postoperative delirium. The study results also revealed a negative correlation between global Lempel-Ziv complexity and delirium severity in broadband (0.1-50 Hz) EEG. Posterior-temporal complexity is also negatively correlated with monocyte chemoattractant protein-1, suggesting that inflammation may play a pathogenic role with delirium precipitation, particularly in posterior regions of the brain that contain important hubs for arousal and consciousness. The work by Tanabe et al. represents an important step in advancing pathophysiologic understanding of delirium and related brain states. With aging surgical populations, the prevalence of delirium and associated complications are likely to increase over the coming years, and this work adds to our knowledge of cortical dynamics underlying delirium. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
