VL  - 12
AU  - Baars, Bernard J
AU  - Geld, Natalie
AU  - Kozma, Robert     AI  - Geld, Natalie; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0983-6002
AU  - Baars, B., Franklin, S., Ramsoy, T., Baars, Baars, B. J., Baars, Baars, B. J., Geld, N., Baars, B...
UR  - http://sfx.mpg.de/sfx_local?sid=OVID:psycdb&id=pmid:&id=doi:10.3389%2Ffpsyg.2021.749868&issn=1664-1078&isbn=&volume=12&issue=&spage=749868&pages=&date=2021&title=Frontiers+in+Psychology&atitle=Global+Workspace+Theory+%28GWT%29+and+prefrontal+cortex%3A+Recent+developments.&aulast=Baars&pid=%3Cauthor%3EBaars%2C+Bernard+J%3BGeld%2C+Natalie%3BKozma%2C+Robert%3C%2Fauthor%3E%3CAN%3E2022-10178-001%3C%2FAN%3E%3CDT%3EJournal+Article%3C%2FDT%3E
LA  - English     PT  - Journal, Peer Reviewed Journal
PB  - ["Frontiers Media S.A.", "Switzerland"]
CY  - ["Baars, Bernard J., baarsbj@gmail.com", "Baars, Bernard J.: baarsbj@gmail.com, Geld, Natalie: ngeld@medneurocme.com"] Switzerland
KW  - *Darwinism
KW  - *Electrical Stimulation
KW  - *Metacognition
KW  - *Prefrontal Cortex
KW  - *Theories
KW  - *Visual Perception
KW  - *Working Space
KW  - *Brain Connectivity
AB  - The article aims to clarify some empirical questions that have been raised, and review evidence that the prefrontal and posterior regions support dynamic global workspace functions, in agreement with several other authors. Static, gross anatomical divisions are superseded by the dynamical connectome of cortex. A wide range of experimental and theoretical studies in the field of consciousness emphasize the complex and rapid interactivity of numerous regions and connectivities in the C-T core. That dynamical view of the C-T core should give us pause about proposing dichotomies that may not be in evidence. From a theoretical viewpoint, Baars et al. suggest that the cortico-thalamic system is inherently dynamic, a view also taken by Pribram, Edelman and Tononi, Freeman et al., Dehaene and Changeux, Edelman et al., and others. If authors view the epicenter of conscious events in cortex to be dynamic rather than anatomically static, the question of prefrontal involvement becomes more nuanced. Most recently, Deco et al. define a functional "rich club" of active cerebral nodes and connectivities that may function as a dynamic global workspace, one that is not rigidly tied to a single anatomical region of cortex. There may be other ways to identify global workspace dynamics, but this appears to be a well-specified candidate. Consciousness studies have been undergoing rapid development, in part, due to new experimental techniques and brain monitoring. The GW "family" of theories has been at the forefront of these developments, and continues to make novel predictions. Relevant mathematical advances are also emerging, including spatio-temporal dynamic network and graph theory approaches. Authors hope that these points will help to clarify the evidence about GWT and the conscious brain. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.749868
