TY  - JOUR
AN  - rayyan-570559887
TI  - A comparison of four dyadic synchronization models.
Y1  - 2021
T2  - Nonlinear Dynamics, Psychology, and Life Sciences
SN  - 1090-0578
VL  - 25
IS  - 1
SP  - 19-39
AU  - Guastello, Stephen J
AU  - Peressini, Anthony F
AU  - Delaherche, E., Chetouani, M., Mahdhaoui, A., Saint-Georges, C., Viaux, S., Cohen, D., Delaherche...
UR  - http://sfx.mpg.de/sfx_local?sid=OVID:psycdb&id=pmid:&id=doi:&issn=1090-0578&isbn=&volume=25&issue=1&spage=19&pages=19-39&date=2021&title=Nonlinear+Dynamics%2C+Psychology%2C+and+Life+Sciences&atitle=A+comparison+of+four+dyadic+synchronization+models.&aulast=Guastello&pid=%3Cauthor%3EGuastello%2C+Stephen+J%3BPeressini%2C+Anthony+F%3C%2Fauthor%3E%3CAN%3E2021-41862-002%3C%2FAN%3E%3CDT%3EJournal+Article%3C%2FDT%3E
LA  - English     PT  - Journal, Peer Reviewed Journal
PB  - ["Society for Chaos Theory in Psychology & Life Sciences", "US"]
CY  - ["Guastello, Stephen J.: Department of Psychology, Marquette University, P. O. Box 1881, Milwaukee, WI, US, 53201, Stephen.Guastello@marquette.edu", "Guastello, Stephen J.: Stephen.Guastello@marquette.edu"] US
KW  - *Autonomic Nervous System
KW  - *Dyads
KW  - *Models
KW  - *Physiological Arousal
KW  - *Teams
KW  - Nonlinear Regression
KW  - Responses
KW  - Synchrony
AB  - Synchronization is a special case of self-organization in which one can observe close mimicry in behavior of the system components. Synchrony in body movements, autonomic arousal, and EEG activity among human individuals has attracted considerable attention for their possible roles in social interaction. This article is specifically concerned with autonomic synchrony and finding the best model for the dyadic relationships, with regard to both theoretical and empirical accuracy, that could be extrapolated to synchrony levels for groups and teams of three or more people. The four models that are compared in this study have different theoretical origins: the two-variable linear regression function, a three- parameter nonlinear regression function, the logistic map function stated in polynomial form, and the logistic map function stated as an exponential regression structure. The data for this study were electrodermal responses collected from a team of four people engaged in an emergency response simulation that produced 12 dyadic time series. Results shows strong levels of fit between the data and all four models, although there were significant differences among them. Further research directions point toward finding conditions that favor one model over another and exploring other possible nonlinear structures. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
