UR  - http://sfx.mpg.de/sfx_local?sid=OVID:psycdb&id=pmid:&id=doi:&issn=0419-4217&isbn=9780438338081&volume=80&issue=1-B%28E%29&spage=No&pages=No+Pagination+Specified&date=2019&title=Dissertation+Abstracts+International%3A+Section+B%3A+The+Sciences+and+Engineering&atitle=Neuroergonomics+of+sensemaking.&aulast=Atilola&pid=%3Cauthor%3EAtilola%2C+Emma+Afua+Ameng%3C%2Fauthor%3E%3CAN%3E2018-52508-163%3C%2FAN%3E%3CDT%3EDissertation%3C%2FDT%3E
LA  - English     PT  - Dissertation Abstract
PB  - ["ProQuest Information & Learning", "US"]
CY  - US
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
KW  - *Air Traffic Control
KW  - *Human Factors Engineering
KW  - Prefrontal Cortex
AB  - Sensemaking is a cognitive process done either consciously or unconsciously to interpret surprises and add meaning to equivocal and ambiguous situations. In order to identify and understand the neural micro-cognitive factors of sensemaking, a better understanding of neuroergonomics, the human brain at work, is needed. Air traffic control affords a domain to study uncertainty, surprises, dynamic problems and tacit mental processing representative of sensemaking in complex or chaotic problem domains. Understanding the contributing factors of workload during sensemaking may afford the detection and mitigation of mental workload associated with sensemaking tasks. The goal of the research was to provide plausible explanations for individual and gender differences in sensemaking by using neural and behavioral performance data. We also sought to identify the areas of the brain responsible for complex and chaos sensemaking tasks. The brain areas responsible for sensemaking were studied by the use of functional near infrared spectroscopy (fNIRS) to investigate oxygen consumption and de-oxygenated data in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC), and electroencephalography (EEG) for brain electrical signals collected from the Brodmann's 8 regions of the frontal cortex. Two-way Analysis of Variance was performed to assess effects of gender and sensemaking problem typology on workload, performance and neurophysiological measures. Research results showed there was no interaction effect for gender and problem typology at alpha of 0.05 when workload, response time, misclassification error, EEG mean power, oxygenated and de-oxygenation hemoglobin were explored. In addition, there was enough evidence of statistically significant problem typology effect when workload and performance data were assessed. The study of de-oxygenated hemoglobin in the DLPFC showed the only statistically significant evidence of gender effect. EEG mean power and de-oxygenated hemoglobin showed statistical evidence of brain region activation differences during sensemaking. Lastly, frontal theta power and delta power were prevalent during the cognitive process of sensemaking. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
ER  -

