UR  - http://sfx.mpg.de/sfx_local?sid=OVID:psycdb&id=pmid:&id=doi:&issn=0419-4217&isbn=9781369706628&volume=78&issue=9-B%28E%29&spage=No&pages=No+Pagination+Specified&date=2018&title=Dissertation+Abstracts+International%3A+Section+B%3A+The+Sciences+and+Engineering&atitle=Brain+organization+and+information+integration.&aulast=Hoel&pid=%3Cauthor%3EHoel%2C+Erik%3C%2Fauthor%3E%3CAN%3E2017-29364-023%3C%2FAN%3E%3CDT%3EDissertation%3C%2FDT%3E
LA  - English     PT  - Dissertation Abstract
PB  - ["ProQuest Information & Learning", "US"]
CY  - US
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
KW  - *Cognitive Processes
KW  - *Biological Neural Networks
KW  - *Brain Connectivity
AB  - Some perspective on how, why, and at which level to construct models of brain function is needed. First, using simple systems, and developing an explicit analysis of causal relationships at different spatiotemporal scales we were able to show, in a principled manner, that causal interactions can be maximal at a macro scale - in other words, that there can be true causal emergence. Again using simple systems, I showed that the capacity to integrate information can peak at a macro scale, demonstrating that macro causal structures can be less reducible than their underlying micro causal structures. The degree of causal emergence in a network depends on its connectivity. In order to investigate the above measures in the brain, first there must exist a model of realistic changes in brain organization, and an examination of what effects on neural activity such organizational changes have. An opportunity comes from the fact that brain development, particularly the fine-tuning of neural circuitry, goes through a critical period (beginning prior to adolescence) when a large amount of restructuring of connectivity is performed (synaptic refinement). I simulated the process of synaptic refinement using a large-scale computer model of the thalamocortical system. Synaptic refinement lead to a reduction in the model's sleep slow wave activity, offering a possible explanation for the observation that, across multiples species, a drop in slow wave activity is seen during adolescence. By showing that changes in organization lead to changes in neural activity, these modeling experiments set the stage for assessing how organization impacts integrated information and thus also at which spatiotemporal scale brain activity is best modeled. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
