AB  - Reviews the books, Phi: A Voyage from the Brain to the Soul by Giulio Tononi (2012) and Consciousness: Confessions of a Romantic Reductionist by Christof Koch (2012). Over the last 12 months, two intriguing books have been published on the theme of consciousness from an extended neuroscientific perspective: Phi: A Voyage from the Brain to the Soul by Giulio Tononi and Consciousness: Confessions of a Romantic Reductionist by Christof Koch. The authors are both leading neuroscientists who made outstanding contributions to the joint fields of consciousness studies and philosophy of mind. In addition to the common interest in consciousness, the reason to jointly review these two books is that their authors have convergent theoretical ideas as to how brain processes can originate conscious states. Specifically, they focus on the concept of information and how it is elaborated by the brain in order to construct a conscious picture of both the internal and external world. But let us now look at each book in more detail. These are all significant implications capable of stimulating fruitful debates in neuroscience. For example, it will be interesting to see whether findings from studies on brain activity across pathologies of consciousness can provide further support to the hypothesis that the saliency of consciousness is directly associated with the maintenance of a certain amount of neurotopographical complexity in circuitries related to information processing. Both Phi: A Voyage from the Brain to the Soul and Consciousness: Confessions of a Romantic Reductionist are among the most original books ever written about human consciousness. The theory of integrated information is still in its infancy. The next and arguably most difficult step will be to quantify the threshold of integrated information where consciousness becomes relevant. This path is certainly worth pursuing, even though Tononi's and Koch's suggestions might require refinements and revisions. Another adventurous voyage into the mysteries of the brain has just begun. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1080/13546805.2013.831615
