AB  - Modern neuroscience has given us intimate knowledge of the brain-its structures, their functions, and their relationship to the rest of the nervous system and the body. And while the areas responsible for intelligence and memory are recognized, few structures can be said with certainty to house the mind, whose existence is viewed by many as a simple state of neural arousal. From this standpoint, a practical understanding of consciousness remains remote. Brain, Mind, and Consciousness argues cogently for a major revision of what we know about the mind-brain dichotomy. Applying new developments in basic and quantum physics to neuroscience, this brief volume offers a new conceptual framework for the neural bases of cognition and behavior, informed by such diverse areas as complexity theory, chaos theory, and relativity. The book's extended illustrations from schizophrenic, dissociative, and epileptiform brain activity address key points in the debate over subjective experience and signal new directions for research and theory. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4614-0436-1
