KW  - *Computers
KW  - *Electroencephalography
KW  - *Neural Plasticity
KW  - *Sleep
KW  - *Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation
KW  - Consciousness States
AB  - Two major questions currently exist in sleep research. First, what is the function of sleep? And second, why does consciousness fade during early non-rapid eye movement (NREM) sleep? Sleep function may be related to the homeostatic regulation of slow wave activity (SWA; electroencephalographic power 0.5-4 Hz). SWA increases with prior wakefulness and declines with sleep. It is also known that SWA increases following learning. These observations can be explained by the hypothesis that SWA is a reflection of synaptic strength and that synaptic strength increases with wakefulness and is homeostatically downscaled during sleep. The research presented in this thesis tests this hypothesis through the use of transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) and computer modeling. General background for this work was provided by developing a computer model of the circuits activated by TMS. Following this, it was demonstrated that trains of TMS pulses can potentiate cortical circuits in human subjects, which produces an increase in SWA. To further explore the relationship between sleep EEG patterns and synaptic strength, computer simulations were used to provide specific predictions about how the sleep EEG should change with a reduction in synaptic strength. Work conducted in parallel with that presented in this thesis has demonstrated that these same changes occur in rats and humans over the course of sleep. Collectively, these results support the hypothesis that SWA reflects synaptic strength, and that synaptic strength is homeostatically regulated during sleep. A recent proposal suggests that the fading of consciousness observed during early NREM sleep is due to a decline of the brain's capacity to integrate information. Using TMS combined with EEG in human subjects, it has been shown that during NREM sleep there are indeed signs of decreased integrated information in the brain. Computer simulations contained in this thesis provide a mechanistic account for these changes. Specifically, these simulations demonstrated that reduced cortical integration arises due to increased synaptic release of GABA during NREM sleep. Furthermore, the model demonstrated that information capacity of the brain declines as a result of widespread brain activation inevitably engaging cortical bistability mechanisms during NREM sleep, leading to a cortical downstate. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
